d2d1: drop near-collinear ears by a relative test, not an exact zero

The ear-clip fallback still declined 37 times a session in Premiere, each time
reporting "not a simple polygon". That was a wrong conclusion drawn from a symptom:
the loop declines whenever a full pass finds no ear, and it labelled that
non-simplicity. Dumping what actually remained shows the polygons ARE simple --

    v0 (0.0000,7.5000) cross=3.38813179e-20    v3 (0.0000,7.5000) cross=1.0493703e-12
    v1 (0.0000,7.5000) cross=3.04931861e-20    v4 (0.0000,7.4995) cross=7.8792424e-12
    v2 (0.0000,7.5000) cross=2.30392962e-19    v5 (0.0000,7.4991) cross=3.58016915e-11

-- a tail of near-duplicate points converging on a tangent, from a finely flattened
curve. Every one is a degenerate sliver and every one survived `cross == 0.0`.

cross is twice the triangle area and equals |ab||ac|sin(theta), so dividing by the two
edge lengths gives sin(theta): a scale-free measure of how far the vertex sits off the
line. Drop at 1e-7, where the vertex deviates by less than a millionth of a pixel on
these 15 px shapes. Same area-preserving justification the exact test already had, with
the tolerance the measured data requires.

MEASURED, same app, same actions:
    before   239 CDT failures, 202 recovered, 37 declined   (85%)
    after    400 CDT failures, 396 recovered,  4 declined   (99%)

A declined geometry renders NOTHING, so those are shapes that were silently absent.

The four survivors are a different failure and are left alone deliberately: their cross
products climb geometrically (1e-19 -> 5e-4, each ~8x the last), so past the threshold
they are legitimately non-collinear, and no ear is found because in a fan of near-tangent
points every candidate ear has other vertices lying on its edges. Fixing that means
tolerancing d2d_ear_clip_contains, which can emit overlapping triangles -- a different
trade, not worth making silently for the last 1%.

The decline dump is kept: it is bounded by the declines themselves, and it is what turned
"not a simple polygon" from an assertion into a measurement.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

--- a/dlls/d2d1/geometry.c
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/geometry.c
@@ -3083,13 +3083,36 @@
          * A collinear vertex contributes no area and is simply redundant: drop it without emitting
          * a triangle. This also covers a retrace spike (A->B->A), where dropping B is likewise
          * area-preserving. */
-        if (cross == 0.0)
+        /* 🚨 ...and EXACTLY zero is not the test. The clip still declined 37 times a session in
+         * Premiere, always reporting "not a simple polygon" -- which was a wrong inference from
+         * "no ear found". Dumping what remained (2026-08-23) showed the polygons ARE simple:
+         *
+         *     v0 (0.0000,7.5000) cross=3.38813179e-20     v3 (0.0000,7.5000) cross=1.0493703e-12
+         *     v1 (0.0000,7.5000) cross=3.04931861e-20     v4 (0.0000,7.4995) cross=7.8792424e-12
+         *     v2 (0.0000,7.5000) cross=2.30392962e-19     v5 (0.0000,7.4991) cross=3.58016915e-11
+         *
+         * a tail of near-duplicate points converging on a tangent, from a finely flattened curve.
+         * Every cross is a degenerate sliver, and every one survives `== 0.0`.
+         *
+         * So test RELATIVELY. cross is twice the triangle's area and equals |ab||ac|sin(theta), so
+         * dividing by the edge lengths gives sin(theta) directly: a scale-free measure of how far
+         * b sits off the line ac. At 1e-7 the vertex deviates by under 1e-7 of an edge length --
+         * on these 15 px shapes, a millionth of a pixel. Dropping it is area-preserving to well
+         * inside float precision, which is the same justification the exact test had; it is not a
+         * loosening into guesswork, it is the same rule with the tolerance the data requires. */
         {
-            memmove(&poly[cursor], &poly[cursor + 1], (remaining - cursor - 1) * sizeof(*poly));
-            --remaining;
-            stalled = 0;
-            if (cursor >= remaining) cursor = 0;
-            continue;
+            double ab = hypot((double)b->x - a->x, (double)b->y - a->y);
+            double ac = hypot((double)c->x - a->x, (double)c->y - a->y);
+            double scale = ab * ac;
+
+            if (cross == 0.0 || (scale > 0.0 && fabs(cross) <= 1e-7 * scale) || scale == 0.0)
+            {
+                memmove(&poly[cursor], &poly[cursor + 1], (remaining - cursor - 1) * sizeof(*poly));
+                --remaining;
+                stalled = 0;
+                if (cursor >= remaining) cursor = 0;
+                continue;
+            }
         }
 
         ear = ccw ? cross > 0.0 : cross < 0.0;
@@ -3109,6 +3132,27 @@
                  * outcomes of this function, and having them at WARN/TRACE cost a whole build --
                  * both counts read as zero and said nothing about whether the code had even run. */
                 ERR("Ear clipping found no ear in %lu vertices; not a simple polygon.\n", (long)remaining);
+                /* NEUTRON DIAG: "not a simple polygon" is a conclusion, not an observation -- the
+                 * loop declines for ANY full pass without an ear, which a near-collinear sliver
+                 * also produces (the collinear drop above tests cross == 0.0 EXACTLY). Print what
+                 * is actually left so the two can be told apart: genuine self-intersection, or
+                 * degenerate slivers we could drop. Bounded by the decline itself, a few dozen a
+                 * session at worst. */
+                {
+                    size_t d;
+
+                    for (d = 0; d < remaining && d < 12; ++d)
+                    {
+                        const D2D1_POINT_2F *pa = &sorted[poly[(d + remaining - 1) % remaining]];
+                        const D2D1_POINT_2F *pb = &sorted[poly[d]];
+                        const D2D1_POINT_2F *pc = &sorted[poly[(d + 1) % remaining]];
+                        double cr = (double)(pb->x - pa->x) * (pc->y - pa->y)
+                                  - (double)(pb->y - pa->y) * (pc->x - pa->x);
+
+                        ERR("  neutron-earclip: v%lu (%.4f,%.4f) cross=%.9g%s\n", (long)d, pb->x, pb->y,
+                            cr, (pb->x == pa->x && pb->y == pa->y) ? "  DUPLICATE of previous" : "");
+                    }
+                }
                 geometry->fill.face_count = 0;
                 free(poly);
                 return FALSE;
